  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 145 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nFriday through Tuesday:\n\nThe upper-level ridge begins to break down heading into Friday \nas an upper-level trough digs southward. This trough looks to \nprogress through the Upper Midwest during the day dragging mid-\nlevel vorticity across the region. Accompanying this trough will\nbe a surface low progged to move from the Dakotas down across \nthe Corn Belt and into central IL/IN through the day. Given the \nlatest mid-range model tracks this will likely keep southern WI \non the cooler side of the system, with the warm sector further \nsouth. However, still looking at low-level WAA and moisture to \nwrap around on the north side of the low to bring increase \nshower and thunderstorm\u00a0chances to our neck of the woods. The \nrainfall amount and duration will depend on which model track \nscenario materializes as a more northern track as seen by GFS \nmembers favors a large rainfall window while a southern track \nfavors a small window given only a glancing chance. While not \nexpecting this activity to amount to much, there is enough \ninstability to support scattered thunderstorms and the potential\nfor some small hail. We will continue to monitor the forecasts \nevolution over the next few days. \n\nBehind this end of the week system, expect northwesterly flow \nto continue through the weekend as the upper-level ridge builds \nback across the region. Expect temps to gradually warm through \nthe start of next week before the upper-level ridge flattens out\nand becomes more zonal. But a generally quiet pattern is \nexpected through the middle of next week as high pressure builds\nacross the region.\n\nWagner",
